  ],
  "text": "A Mars landing takes about seven minutes. The signal takes longer than that to reach us. So it is over before anyone here knows it began. Nobody flies it. Nobody can. There is no way to intervene at any point. It arrives at about twenty thousand kilometres an hour, into an atmosphere far too thin to help much. Thin enough that a parachute alone will not slow you enough to survive the landing. So it uses the heat shield, then the parachute, then throws both away and fires rockets. Then the strangest part. The rocket stage hovers and lowers the rover on cables. Because landing legs would kick up dust and dig into ground nobody has surveyed. The cables are cut, and the stage flies off to crash somewhere safely distant. All of it happened before we heard about the first step. The software works, or the mission ends. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
